{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Units.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GroupWithZero.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.twoRow_numerator_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.twoRow_numerator_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.lean#L102-L122","doc":"**The two-row condition has exactly two roots.**\n\nOver a domain and for `q ≠ 0`, `P(z) = 0` iff `z = q` or `(q²+1)·z = q³` — the\nsecond being `z_D = q³/(q²+1)` written division-free. This is what upgrades the\nbracket from \"the two functionals I enumerated\" to a completeness statement\nabout the admissible set. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.two_roots_distinct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.two_roots_distinct","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.lean#L95-L100","doc":"The two roots are distinct: `q` is not `q³/(q²+1)` for `q ≠ 0`, stated\ndivision-free as `q·(q²+1) ≠ q³`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.twoRowNumerator","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.twoRowNumerator","kind":"def","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.lean#L91-L93","doc":"Numerator of the `(1,1,1)` Markov weight, `P(z) = -q²(q - z)(q²z + z - q³)`.\nIts vanishing is the two-row condition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.traceValues_eq_augmentationValues_at_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.traceValues_eq_augmentationValues_at_q","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.lean#L76-L89","doc":"**At `z = q` the Markov trace agrees with the augmentation on every basis\nelement.**\n\nThe four values are the trace's, in increasing length: `1` on the identity, `z`\non each generator, `z²` on the two length-two elements, `z·(h·z+1)` on the\nlongest. The augmentation `σ ↦ q` gives `q ^ ℓ(w)`. At `z = q` the first three\nmatch by inspection and the fourth is `markovTrace_longest_at_q`, so the trace\nat that root *is* the augmentation rather than a third admissible functional —\nwhich is what closes the admissible set at two elements. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.markovTrace_longest_at_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.markovTrace_longest_at_q","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.lean#L64-L74","doc":"**At `z = q` the longest element's trace is `q³`** — the augmentation's\nvalue there.\n\nThis is the load-bearing identity behind \"the second root of the two-row\ncondition is not a third functional\". It is exactly where `h·q = q² - 1` is\nused; with any other `h` the two would differ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.trLongest","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.trLongest","kind":"def","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.lean#L60-L62","doc":"The Ocneanu trace's value on the longest basis element `σ₀σ₁σ₀`, as a\nfunction of the loop parameter: `tr(T_{w₀}) = z·(h·z + 1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.hOf_mul_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.hOf_mul_q","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.lean#L54-L58","doc":"`h·q = q² - 1`: the one place the Hecke relation enters below. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.hOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.hOf","kind":"def","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TwoRowRootsAndAugmentation.lean#L51-L52","doc":"The Hecke parameter `h = q - q⁻¹`, with `q` a unit. ","signatureChunk":0}]}